<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ruleset name="smith-java"
         xmlns="http://pmd.sourceforge.net/ruleset/2.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://pmd.sourceforge.net/ruleset/2.0.0 https://pmd.sourceforge.io/ruleset_2_0_0.xsd">

  <description>
    The java rules that name a defect, and nothing else.

    Until 2026-08-22 this was `rulesets/java/quickstart.xml`, PMD's kitchen sink. Measured over a
    150-file slice of a real SAP Commerce codebase it fired 36 distinct rules, 244 findings, 13.15
    per 1000 lines — against 14 findings from everything in `rules/*.yaml` put together. PMD was
    95% of what a developer read, and all 82 of its criticals came from three style rules:
    GuardLogStatement, FinalParameterInAbstractMethod and AvoidUsingVolatile. A push was blocked
    from merging by a missing `if (LOG.isDebugEnabled())` and by `final` on an interface parameter,
    where `final` does nothing at all.

    Every rule below reported a defect on that corpus. The rules that survived are listed one by
    one rather than by category: a category is a promise about code nobody has measured, and the
    counts in the comments are the evidence each rule earned its place with.

    A rule added here needs its one-line fix in `pmd-fixes.json`, beside this file. PMD's own text
    names the defect and never the change, and a finding that does not say what to type is half a
    finding. The suite fails naming any rule the two files disagree about.
  </description>

  <!-- errorprone: the four rules that fired on real code. The rest of the category is dropped,
       measured — pulling it whole (with multithreading, without codestyle) reports 178 findings,
       9.60 per 1000, and 139 of them are AvoidDuplicateLiterals, AvoidCatchingGenericException,
       ReplaceJavaUtilDate and UseConcurrentHashMap. Those are house opinions, not defects. -->
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/CloseResource"/>                                <!-- 5 -->
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/EmptyCatchBlock"/>                              <!-- 1 -->
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/MissingStaticMethodInNonInstantiatableClass"/>  <!-- 1 -->
  <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/UseLocaleWithCaseConversions"/>                 <!-- 1 -->

  <!-- bestpractices: the nine that named something wrong. GuardLogStatement lives here too and is
       deliberately absent — 53 findings, the single loudest rule in the review, and a guarded log
       call is a micro-optimisation, not a bug. -->
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/LiteralsFirstInComparisons"/>  <!-- 3 -->
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/UnusedFormalParameter"/>       <!-- 3 -->
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/UseStandardCharsets"/>         <!-- 3 -->
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/PreserveStackTrace"/>          <!-- 2 -->
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/DoubleBraceInitialization"/>   <!-- 2 -->
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/UnusedPrivateField"/>          <!-- 2 -->
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/NonExhaustiveSwitch"/>         <!-- 1 -->
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/UnusedLocalVariable"/>         <!-- 1 -->
  <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/MissingOverride"/>             <!-- 1 -->

  <!-- codestyle: dropped whole. 100 of the 244 findings and not one of them a defect —
       UnnecessaryImport 34 (the same statement as `no-star-import`, which this repository deleted
       for being 30% of the output and zero defects), FinalParameterInAbstractMethod 28,
       TypeParameterNamingConventions 19, UselessParentheses 16. Formatting is a formatter's job. -->

  <!-- multithreading: dropped whole. Its only critical was AvoidUsingVolatile, and `volatile` is
       the correct tool often enough that flagging it blocks more good changes than bad ones.
       DoNotUseThreads fires 6 times on a platform whose own APIs hand you threads. -->

  <!-- design: dropped whole. Cyclomatic complexity, coupling and law-of-Demeter counts are a
       conversation to have about a module, not a line to change in a diff. -->

  <!-- documentation: dropped whole. Comment size and javadoc presence are not review findings. -->

  <!-- performance: dropped whole. Every rule in it is a guess about a hot path the analyzer cannot
       see; on this corpus the ones quickstart ships fired only on code nobody profiles. -->

  <!-- security: dropped, though it is only HardCodedCryptoKey and InsecureCryptoIv. Both are real
       defects and both fired zero times on 18,550 lines, so there is no evidence either is precise
       here. Add them back with a corpus measurement, not with a hunch. -->

</ruleset>
